Tensile (ROCmSoftwarePlatform/Tensile commit
7449c7fefd3d208ba6a2705699cd1c13b654ad87, the tag rocBLAS's own
tensile_tag.txt pins at rocm-6.4.4): force WorkGroupMapping=1 for gfx803
assembly solutions at the point every solution's parameters get
normalized, instead of rewriting the WGM value in every Tensile Logic
*.yaml file (wgm-miscompute.sh, kept alongside this patch, not removed --
see that script's own header for the full hardware measurements this is
based on; not repeated here).

WHY
---
Same finding, different mechanism -- see
patches/rocblas/wgm-miscompute.sh's own header for the hardware
measurements (RX 470, ROCm 6.4.4) this is based on; not a
re-investigation. Unlike the root repo's rocm7 line, where Tensile is
vendored directly in the rocm-libraries monorepo checkout
(shared/tensile/), 6.4.4-era rocBLAS pip-installs Tensile from its own
standalone repo at CMake configure time (CMakeLists.txt's
virtualenv_install("git+https://github.com/${tensile_fork}/Tensile.git@${tensile_tag}"),
tensile_tag read from rocBLAS's own tensile_tag.txt) -- there is no
in-tree Tensile checkout to COPY a patch onto before the build starts.
wgm-miscompute-source.sh works around this by cloning the exact pinned
Tensile commit into its own local directory, patching it there, and
pointing rocBLAS's existing Tensile_TEST_LOCAL_PATH cache variable
(rmake.py's --cmake-darg passthrough, see the driver) at that local copy
instead -- rocBLAS's own CMakeLists.txt already prefers a local path over
the pip-fetch when Tensile_TEST_LOCAL_PATH is set, so no change to
rocBLAS's own build files is needed.

NOT YET RE-VERIFIED as behaviorally equivalent to wgm-miscompute.sh on
real hardware for THIS line -- the equivalence check (identical generated
kernel names, zero *_WGM8 symbols) was run against the rocm7 line's pinned
Tensile source (rocm-libraries @ cd9574023093742434e8c992d13b89ab9a6c1cf8,
confirmed identical between the two mechanisms there), not re-run against
this line's separately-pinned Tensile commit. The anchor text this patch
targets (the `if state["WorkGroupMapping"] == -1: ... = 1` block) is
byte-for-byte identical between the two Tensile sources at their
respective pins, confirmed by direct comparison, so this is expected to
behave the same way -- but "expected" is not "verified" per this repo's
own standard. wgm-miscompute.sh remains the one actually wired into
rocm6.4.4/Dockerfile pending that confirmation.

Also NOT YET established whether the underlying WGM!=1 assembly-codegen
defect is gfx803-specific or shared with other GCN-family architectures
-- see wgm-miscompute-source.patch's counterpart in the root repo's
patches/rocblas/ for the same open question and the working assumption
(gfx803-specific is more likely, per Schaka, since more heavily-used
architectures would plausibly have already surfaced a bug this blunt) --
not repeated here.

WHAT
----
Identical transformation to the rocm7 line's patch of the same name,
applied against this line's separately-pinned Tensile source (same
anchor text, different line number: 3186 here vs. 3210 in the
rocm-libraries monorepo copy -- confirmed by direct comparison, not
assumed identical).

Applies to: Tensile @ ROCmSoftwarePlatform/Tensile commit
7449c7fefd3d208ba6a2705699cd1c13b654ad87 (rocBLAS's own tensile_tag.txt
pin at rocm-6.4.4).

diff --git a/Tensile/SolutionStructs.py b/Tensile/SolutionStructs.py
index 26efe1a6..c3bb67f4 100644
--- a/Tensile/SolutionStructs.py
+++ b/Tensile/SolutionStructs.py
@@ -3187,6 +3187,21 @@ class Solution(collections.abc.Mapping):
       # -1 code is same as 1. Convert to 1.
       state["WorkGroupMapping" ] = 1

+    # GFX803_WGM_MISCOMPUTE_PATCH: on gfx803 (GCN3/Polaris), the assembly
+    # kernel's WorkGroupMapping!=1 swizzle (blockId = wg1 / WGM, then a
+    # magic-number-divide re-deal across a WGM-wide tile block) does not
+    # produce a bijection between workgroups and output tiles -- several
+    # workgroups land on the same tile while others are never claimed, so
+    # the GEMM silently returns wrong results with rocblas_status_success.
+    # WGM only ever permutes which workgroup claims which tile (the grid
+    # and tile set are unchanged), so WorkGroupMapping=1 (identity) is
+    # always a valid, correct assignment -- forcing it here costs some L2
+    # locality on large GEMMs and nothing else. See
+    # patches/rocblas/wgm-miscompute.sh for the hardware measurements
+    # this is based on.
+    if tuple(state["ISA"]) == (8, 0, 3) and state["KernelLanguage"] == "Assembly":
+      state["WorkGroupMapping" ] = 1
+
     # avoid bug somehow related to GlobalSplitU + Persistent
     # avoid bug related to WGM<0
     # General Batch doesn't support PersistentKernel
